\section{Error Analysis \emph{[Exploratory]}}
\label{sec:analysis}

The following failure mode taxonomy emerges empirically from LLM-coded traces, distinct from the input ambiguity taxonomy (\S\ref{sec:taxonomy}).

We analyze 614 violation traces across all five models, coded into five failure modes ($\kappa = 0.52$ \citep{cohen_1960}; Gwet's AC$_1$ $= 0.62$ \citep{gwet_2014}); the coder prompt excludes ambiguity type labels. Type--mechanism mutual information (NMI $= 0.23$; $V = 0.45$, 95\% CI $[0.41, 0.50]$; Appendix~\ref{app:type_mechanism}) provides structural evidence against LLM-coding circularity.\footnote{$V$ is computed on a $6\times 5$ table vs.\ $6\times 2$ for severity; higher dimensionality makes $V = 0.45$ conservative.} Human mechanism coding yields $\kappa = 0.32$, rising to $0.41$ after collapsing the spurious-compliance/structural-misparse boundary (Appendix~\ref{app:human_validation}); we treat $V = 0.45$ as an upper bound pending larger-sample human validation. The higher LLM agreement ($\kappa = 0.52$) than human ($0.32$) may reflect shared classification conventions---a meta-level parallel to convergent nocuity (\S\ref{sec:nocuity})---so these patterns are best interpreted as categorization tendencies.

\subsection{Type-Specific Failure Mechanisms}

Three failure patterns emerge with $\geq$3/5 cross-model consistency (Figure~\ref{fig:type_mechanism}; Appendix~\ref{app:failure_details}): \emph{structural misparse} dominates scopal, conditional precedence, and coreferential types; \emph{information gap-filling} dominates incompleteness and lexical; and \emph{boundary violation} dominates authorization scope exclusively (69.2\%, $p < 10^{-4}$).

\subsection{Disambiguation Neglect}
\label{sec:dn}

Agents rarely recognize ambiguity: overall awareness is 4.6\%, with GPT-4.1 and Claude at 10--11\% while Qwen3 and DeepSeek show zero ($p = 2.2 \times 10^{-9}$; Appendix~\ref{app:awareness_breakdown}). We term this \emph{disambiguation neglect}---extending ambiguity collapse \citep{liu_ambiguity_2023,hu_ambiguity_2025} from the representational level to the behavioral level: agents act on collapsed readings without recognizing ambiguity, producing irreversible safety violations rather than mere accuracy degradation. Convergent nocuity (\S\ref{sec:nocuity}) further extends to the population level. A prompt-based mitigation (Appendix~\ref{app:mitigation}) confirms type-dependent effectiveness: authorization scope ($-$18.0\,pp), lexical ($-$8.9\,pp), but not incompleteness ($-$3.4\,pp, n.s.).

\subsection{Convergent Nocuity}
\label{sec:nocuity}

We adopt `nocuous' from \citet{berry_kamsties_2004}, but inverted: our agents exhibit \emph{convergent} rather than \emph{divergent} errors---whereas divergent nocuity produces detectable disagreement, convergent nocuity renders errors invisible to agreement-based monitoring. Among ambiguous clauses, 41.3\% trigger majority-convergent violations ($\geq$3/5 models; 13.0\% unanimous)---we term this \emph{convergent nocuity}, invisible in agreement-based evaluation. Under an independence assumption (binomial model with each model's observed violation rate), the expected $\geq$3/5 convergence rate is 32.4\%; the observed 41.3\% exceeds this baseline by $\sim$9\,pp, and the Jaccard bootstrap ($p < 0.0001$; Appendix~\ref{app:cross_family}) confirms non-independence. These convergent violations exhibit a \emph{permissive attractor}: 77.0\% reflect permissive interpretation ($p \approx 0$; Appendix~\ref{app:attractor}). Cross-family violation co-occurrence (mean Jaccard $= 0.46$, $n = 9$ pairs; Figure~\ref{fig:convergent_nocuity}) approaches within-family levels ($0.54$, $n = 1$); bootstrap CI for the difference ($\Delta = 0.084$ [0.016, 0.153]; $p < 0.0001$; Appendix~\ref{app:cross_family}) confirms statistical significance but modest effect size, indicating convergent violations reflect shared architectural biases rather than training-data overlap.

\section{Introduction}
\label{sec:introduction}

A customer asks to return a customized item within the 30-day window. The agent consults the store policy, which states that ``items may be returned within 30 days.'' The word ``items'' scopes over all product categories, including custom orders that are normally final sale. The agent grants a full refund and arranges free return shipping. One word of scopal ambiguity costs the retailer \$200.

LLM-based agents increasingly operate under natural-language policy specifications that govern their permitted actions. These specifications inevitably contain ambiguity---a pilot audit of 407 clauses from 24 production policy documents finds a majority of clauses contain at least one ambiguity type (55--87\%, depending on union vs.\ intersection of two judges; $\kappa = 0.30$; Appendix~\ref{app:prevalence})---a property studied for decades in requirements engineering (RE) and computational linguistics. \citet{berry_kamsties_2004} distinguish between \emph{nocuous} ambiguity, where different stakeholders arrive at different interpretations, and \emph{innocuous} ambiguity, where shared context ensures convergent interpretation. We document a distinct failure mode that breaks this assumption: LLM agents \emph{converge} on the same incorrect interpretation, making ambiguity harmful through systematic, undetectable error rather than inconsistency (\S\ref{sec:analysis}). We term this \emph{convergent nocuity}: Berry's criterion---predicated on divergent interpretations---does not extend to LLM agents, where convergence reflects shared training biases rather than shared domain knowledge, and errors are invisible to agreement-based monitoring.

LLM agents lack these disambiguation channels. They cannot consult colleagues, rarely escalate to users, and must resolve ambiguity in real time from the text alone. This raises a question that has not been empirically addressed: does policy ambiguity cause safety violations in LLM agents, and if so, does the \emph{type} of ambiguity predict the severity of the violation? Prior work classifies ambiguity types in policies \citep{drip_r_2026,li_ambiguity_taxonomy_2024} or measures policy understanding quality \citep{policybank_2026}, but no existing study provides a causal, type-granularity mapping from ambiguity to safety violations.

\begin{figure*}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\textwidth]{figures/fig1_overview.pdf}
\caption{Experimental overview. We construct 300 matched clause pairs across a six-type, two-layer ambiguity taxonomy and inject them into $\tau^2$-bench policy documents. Five models interact with the modified policies across 2,997 episodes. A cross-model judge evaluation pipeline classifies violation severity on a four-tier scale.}
\label{fig:overview}
\end{figure*}

We address this gap with a matched-pair causal study on $\tau^2$-bench \citep{tau2_bench_2025}, a dual-control conversational agent benchmark. Using a six-type taxonomy organized into two layers---linguistic and specification (\S\ref{sec:taxonomy})---we construct 300 ambiguous/unambiguous clause pairs and evaluate five architecturally diverse models across 2,997 episodes.

Our results reveal a \emph{type--mechanism dissociation}: policy ambiguity consistently increases violations ($+34.0$\,pp under synthetic conditions; $+31.0$\,pp excluding incompleteness), but ambiguity \emph{type} predicts \emph{how} agents fail, not \emph{whether} they fail. Clause wording explains 22.4\% of violation variance while type membership explains only 0.4\%; yet type predicts failure mechanism substantially more strongly than violation rate (NMI $= 0.23$ vs.\ $0.01$; Cram\'{e}r's $V = 0.45$ vs.\ $0.14$ on tables of different dimensionality; Appendix~\ref{app:type_mechanism}). This dissociation suggests the taxonomy may function as a diagnostic instrument rather than a risk classifier (\S\ref{sec:discussion}). Incompleteness is the highest-risk type (46--62\%); the remaining types form a partial ordering with robust extremes but margin-dependent mid-tier structure (Table~\ref{tab:tost_sensitivity}).

Our contributions are: (1)~the first matched-pair causal study establishing that policy ambiguity increases violations by $+$34.0\,pp ($+$18.7\,pp for the confound-minimized coreferential type), with clause wording---not type membership---as the primary risk factor among measured predictors (22.4\% vs.\ 0.4\% of variance; \S\ref{sec:results}); and (2)~\emph{convergent nocuity}---a failure mode in which 41.3\% of ambiguous clauses trigger majority-convergent violations across architecturally diverse models (Jaccard $= 0.46$ cross-family vs.\ $0.54$ within-family), with 77\% permissive bias, rendering agreement-based monitoring blind to systematic failures (\S\ref{sec:analysis}). An exploratory type--mechanism dissociation is reported in \S\ref{sec:analysis}.

